Make mupen64plus's RDRAM model work with libdragon's IPL3.

A ROM built by `pak link` uses libdragon's IPL3, which does not boot on stock
mupen64plus 2.5.9. Two independent bugs in its RDRAM emulation, both found by
instrumenting it against a Pak ROM. Neither is Pak's, and neither can be
worked around from inside a ROM.

1. idfield_value() takes the wrong byte.

   RDRAM registers are stored byte-swapped ("RDRAM registers are physically
   little-endian" -- libdragon boot/rdram.c). Decoding DEVICE_ID back to a
   device id, mupen64plus reads id bits 0..5, 6 and 15 from the right places
   but takes bits 7..14 from the byte-swapped word's bits 16..23 instead of
   8..15. It therefore cannot match any device parked at an id above 63,
   which is exactly what libdragon does before probing (INITIAL_ID = 511), so
   get_module() misses and every register read returns 0.

2. The Current-Control corruption window closes too early.

   read_rdram_dram_corrupted() exists so a bootcode's current-calibration
   sweep sees unreliable RDRAM at low current levels -- without it the sweep
   finds every level equally perfect, computes a weighted sum of zero, and
   concludes the chip is dead. mupen64plus opens that window on a broadcast
   write to DELAY and closes it on a broadcast write to MODE. That matches
   Nintendo's IPL3, which calibrates between the two. libdragon broadcasts
   MODE early, to enable every chip up front, and calibrates afterwards -- so
   the window is already shut when the sweep runs.

   Keyed instead on what the write means: calibration is in progress while a
   MODE write leaves the chip in MANUAL current mode, and over once it is put
   into AUTO. That is per-chip and self-limiting, so dynarec's fast memory is
   restored after each chip exactly as before.

Together these are the difference between "IPL3 detected 64 MB of RDRAM" plus
`reserved opcode: 80000300:1` and a ROM that boots. (The 64 MB message is
unrelated noise -- mupen64plus peeks at CPU register $s4 on the assumption
that Nintendo's IPL3 keeps the size there. It is a DebugMessage and stops
nothing. This patch deliberately leaves it alone.)

Verified against mupen64plus-core 2.5.9+341+gf82b37bf. Before: stage 2 is
DMA'd to 0x00FFFF08 (memsize == 0) and the payload never loads. After: stage 2
goes to 0x007FFF08 (8 MB detected) and the payload arrives --
`PI cart=10001000 -> dram=00000400 len=11BAC`.

--- a/src/device/rdram/rdram.c
+++ b/src/device/rdram/rdram.c
@@ -59,7 +59,7 @@
 {
     return ((((device_id >> 26) & 0x3f) <<  0)
           | (((device_id >> 23) & 0x01) <<  6)
-          | (((device_id >> 16) & 0xff) <<  7)
+          | (((device_id >>  8) & 0xff) <<  7)
           | (((device_id >>  7) & 0x01) << 15));
 }
 
@@ -195,12 +195,23 @@
         map_corrupt_rdram(rdram, 1);
     }
 
-    /* HACK: Detect when current Control calibration is over,
-     * so we can restore the original rdram_dram handler
-     * and let dynarec have it's fast_memory enabled.
+    /* Current-Control calibration is in progress whenever a MODE write leaves
+     * the chip in MANUAL current mode, and over once it is put into AUTO. Key
+     * the corrupted-read handler on that rather than on a broadcast MODE
+     * write: a bootcode is free to broadcast MODE before it calibrates (
+     * libdragon's does, to enable every chip up front), and keying off the
+     * broadcast ends the window before the calibration sweep even starts --
+     * so every current level reads back perfect, the sweep finds no
+     * difference between them, and the bootcode concludes the chip is dead.
+     *
+     * AUTO is bit 7 of the logical register; RDRAM registers are stored
+     * byte-swapped, so it is bit 31 of the value written here.
      */
+    if (reg == RDRAM_MODE_REG) {
+        map_corrupt_rdram(rdram, (value & UINT32_C(0x80000000)) ? 0 : 1);
+    }
+
     if (address & RDRAM_BCAST_ADDRESS_MASK && reg == RDRAM_MODE_REG) {
-        map_corrupt_rdram(rdram, 0);
 
         /* HACK: In the IPL3 procedure, at this point,
          * the amount of detected memory can be found in s4 */
